; The two ways a multi-rule macro is written wrong (MAC-LANG-14,
; 2026-08-15), one refusal each:
;
;   sameArity   two rules taking the same number of arguments. Arity
;               is what SELECTS a rule, so the second is unreachable -
;               a program someone wrote expecting it to run. First
;               match wins, which makes the behaviour defined;
;               refusing at the macro's own line makes it visible,
;               which is the choice this repository makes wherever it
;               has made it. `AX3020`, the code a duplicate PARAMETER
;               already draws, because both say "this macro declares
;               the same thing twice"
;   the call    an invocation whose arity matches no rule. The
;               diagnostic names EVERY arity the macro offers -
;               `takes 1 or 3 arguments` - where the single-rule form
;               could only ever name one, and an author who wrote two
;               rules would otherwise be told what the first one takes
;               and left to guess about the second
;
; Both are at the place the reader can act on: the macro for the
; unreachable rule, the invocation for the arity that matched none.
(data D (A))

(macro sameArity ((sameArity T)
   (:: (syntax/join p T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join p T)) 1))
  ((sameArity U)
   (:: (syntax/join q U) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join q U)) 2)))

(macro spread ((spread T)
   (:: (syntax/join r T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join r T)) 1))
  ((spread T a b)
   (:: (syntax/join r T) Int)
   (fn ((syntax/join r T)) 3)))

(sameArity D)

(spread D 9)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
